  <reporting-org ref="NL-KVK-40409352" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Netherlands Red Cross</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Water the Heart of Climate Action in Uganda (WHCA): Accelerating and scaling up water action to reduce risks and increase climate resilience</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Communities most at risk for climate change and water-related crises</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project addresses climate change and water related risks in Uganda by achieving the following outcomes:
1. Functional Water-related risk knowledge and governance that can guide decision-making
2. Climate, weather and water impact-based forecasting products are available for decision-making
3. Most at-risk communities receive and understand water-related early warning messages
4. Enhanced anticipatory action and adaptation reduce water-related risks
5. Consortum partners collaboration assures interlinkages, quality, inclusion and learning.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project targets the following areas and communities that are at high risk to flooding and/or drought:
- Kamuli and Kayunga (flood prone areas)
- Nakasongola and Pakwach (drought prone areas that also experience flooding).</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project is part of a 5-year programme of Netherlands Red Cross, Climate Centre, IFRC, SOFF, UNDRR and WMO that aims to accelerate and scale up water action to mitigate the impacts of water related risks and increase the climate resilience of affected communities by bringing together different disciplines in the water, climate and disaster management sectors at the local, national and global level. These partners are well positioned to integrate their ways of working and collaborate with governments and communities to reduce climate vulnerability for end-to-end community-centered Early Warning Systems. In Uganda, the WHCA project aims to increase the resilience of communities impacted by water related disasters such as foods and drought in Kamuli, Kayunga, Nakasongola and Pakwach.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Uganda is mostly affected by both hydrological and meteorological hazards (including, among others, floods and droughts), which are further exacerbated by the effects of climate change and cause displacement and severe loss of life, health, property and livelihoods. Uganda is highly vulnerable to the impacts of climate change with a rank of 173 (out of 185) in the ND-GAIN index. INFORM Climate Change 2024 indicates a possible large increase in the risk level and vulnerability in Uganda in the future, if no measures are taken.</narrative>
  </description>
